good afternoon i'm steve kresovich and i  serve as director  of the usaid feed the future innovation  lab for crop improvement  i'm also the moderator of this session  besides my overview we'll also have four  technical presentations given by key  leaders of the program  our innovation lab for crop improvement  was established in september of 2019.  it's a five-year program funded at the  base level of 25 million dollars  we soon will have additional associate  awards either funded by the bureau of  resilience and food security in  washington d.c or in-country programs  our partners for the innovation lab  include cornell  clemson colorado state  kansas state cultural practice and  macquarie university  compared to the scope and objectives of  many of the other feed the future  innovation labs our work is a bit  upstream with an emphasis on both  development of cutting edge  science and thoughtful delivery it also  is predominantly science and not  commodity based  our mission is to equip network  scientists and stakeholders with both  the intellectual and operational  resources to connect breeding efforts  with demand for better support of  smallholder farmers as well as an  attempt to reduce malnutrition and  hunger  our focus is to support efforts  addressing global food security in the  broadest context we were founded on the  development and deployment of scientific  insights and training  tools technologies and methods  our goal is to listen to  learn from and support national  agricultural research institutes as they  establish their own goals and drive  advancement to breed brazilian crop  varieties we believe that national  programs play  the central role in designing  innovations that prioritize  targets for their unique needs  and ultimately hope that this approach  will be more sustainable and reliable  the target crops of the innovation left  for crop improvement include  roots tubers and banana  legumes  excluding soybean and groundnut  all the types of millets and sorghum  we have a broad range of crop mandates  with this broad range of  responsibilities  many of our advances  ultimately will be crop independent  that is we hope to develop tools  technologies and methods for all  this slide highlights a recent summary  of our philosophy and activities to  emphasize the scope of our innovation  lab is broader than solely plant  breeding we take a transdisciplinary  systems oriented view for engagement  as noted previously we're also  grassroots driven that is the directors  of our international centers of  innovation which will be highlighted  subsequently are operational leaders of  the program  the breadth of our crop improvement  continuum addresses additional critical  engagements including resilience and  risk management  that can be as affected by climate or  economic conditions  we also strive for gender equity  and engagement  looking for inclusion  youth participation and ultimately  integrating all these priorities with  improved nutrition and food safety  beyond development and deployment of  scientific insights tools technologies  and methods  we're interested in advancing and  translating research into a public good  again we address key changes affecting  global food security  we strive for outputs and outcomes that  support broader societal priorities in  the 21st century  our approach and continuum of activities  are highlighted in this slide first  we've identified and established a  global network of experts these experts  come together to develop and deploy  scientific insights tools technologies  and methods  driven to the goals and objectives of  the regional leadership of the centers  of innovation  from there  we work with additional partners to  promote healthy crop improvement systems  so it's a systems approach not just  seeds or a new variety and ultimately  the goal is to improve food security  while prioritizing  engagement with a focus on gender youth  nutrition and resilience  it's 